---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "@/components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "@/components/Footer.astro";
import CalculatorBreadcrumb from "@/components/CalculatorBreadcrumb.astro";
import {
  AIR_GAP_MINIMUM_IN,
  AIR_GAP_MINIMUM_NEAR_WALL_IN,
  AIR_GAP_MULTIPLIER,
  AIR_GAP_MULTIPLIER_NEAR_WALL,
  BACKFLOW_APPLICATIONS,
  BACKFLOW_ASSEMBLIES,
  BACKFLOW_DIRECTIONS,
  BACKFLOW_HAZARD_CLASSES,
  MINIMUM_AIR_GAPS,
  fmt,
} from "@/lib/plumbing";

/**
 * Every table on this page is rendered straight out of `src/lib/plumbing.ts`.
 * There are no transcribed copies here, so the page cannot drift from the
 * data — the same discipline the electrical `nec-tables` page uses. Anything
 * needing a comparison operator is precomputed below, because the Astro
 * compiler reads a bare `<=` inside a template expression as an opening tag.
 */
const highHazardBackpressure = BACKFLOW_ASSEMBLIES.filter(
  (a) => a.hazard === "high" && a.backpressure,
);
const siphonageOnly = BACKFLOW_ASSEMBLIES.filter((a) => !a.backpressure);
const notContinuous = BACKFLOW_ASSEMBLIES.filter((a) => !a.continuousPressure);

const sections = [
  { id: "backpressure-vs-backsiphonage", label: "The two directions" },
  { id: "hazard", label: "Hazard class" },
  { id: "assemblies", label: "The assemblies" },
  { id: "air-gaps", label: "Minimum air gaps" },
  { id: "applications", label: "Common applications" },
  { id: "testing", label: "Testing & approval" },
];

const title = "Backflow Prevention Reference — IPC 608 Assemblies & Air Gaps | TradesQuote";
const description =
  "Free backflow prevention reference: backpressure versus backsiphonage, pollution versus contamination hazard, every assembly from air gap to RPZ with what it does and does not protect against, minimum air gaps, and the device each common connection takes.";

const pageUrl = "https://www.tradesquote.ai/calculators/plumbing/backflow-prevention";

const breadcrumbData = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  itemListElement: [
    { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.tradesquote.ai/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Calculators", item: "https://www.tradesquote.ai/calculators" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Plumbing", item: "https://www.tradesquote.ai/calculators/plumbing" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", position: 4, name: "Backflow Prevention", item: pageUrl },
  ],
};
---

<Layout {title} {description}>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <link rel="canonical" href={pageUrl} />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="Backflow Prevention Reference — IPC 608 Assemblies & Air Gaps" />
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:url" content={pageUrl} />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="TradesQuote" />
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://www.tradesquote.ai/logo.svg" />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
    <meta name="twitter:title" content="Backflow Prevention Reference | TradesQuote" />
    <meta name="twitter:description" content={description} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbData)} />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="bg-white min-h-screen text-slate-700">
    <Header />

    <main>
      <!-- ==== HERO ==== -->
      <section class="relative overflow-hidden bg-gradient-to-b from-slate-50 to-white">
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pt-10 pb-8">
          <CalculatorBreadcrumb
            category="Plumbing"
            categoryHref="/calculators/plumbing"
            current="Backflow Prevention"
          />

          <div class="max-w-3xl">
            <span
              class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full bg-slate-200 px-3 py-1 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-600"
            >
              Plumbing Reference · IPC 608 · Free
            </span>
            <h1 class="mt-4 text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900">
              Backflow Prevention Reference
            </h1>
            <p class="mt-5 text-lg text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
              Choosing an assembly is two questions, in this order: <strong>which direction</strong> can
              backflow happen here, and <strong>how bad</strong> is what would come back. Get the direction
              wrong and nothing else helps — because
              <strong>no vacuum breaker of any kind protects against backpressure</strong>, whatever its name
              suggests. That one fact rules out{" "}
              {siphonageOnly.length} of the {BACKFLOW_ASSEMBLIES.length} devices below the moment a boiler, a
              pump, or an injector is involved.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
            {
              sections.map((s) => (
                <a
                  href={`#${s.id}`}
                  class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white px-3.5 py-2 text-sm font-semibold text-slate-600 hover:border-orange-500 hover:text-orange-600 transition"
                >
                  {s.label}
                </a>
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ==== THE TWO DIRECTIONS ==== -->
      <section id="backpressure-vs-backsiphonage" class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900">
          The two directions backflow happens
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
          Every device on this page stops backsiphonage. Only some stop backpressure, and that is the split
          that decides most jobs. Work out which is physically possible at the connection before looking at
          anything else.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-8 grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5">
          {
            BACKFLOW_DIRECTIONS.map((d) => (
              <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
                <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900">{d.label}</h3>
                <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">{d.description}</p>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>

        <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-amber-200 bg-amber-50 p-6">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-amber-900">The mistake worth naming</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-amber-900 leading-relaxed">
            A vacuum breaker works by letting air in when pressure drops. Push water at it from below and the
            air inlet is held <em>shut</em> by the very pressure it is supposed to relieve, so it passes
            backflow straight through. The {siphonageOnly.length} devices with "vacuum breaker" in the name —{" "}
            {siphonageOnly.map((a) => a.abbr).join(", ")} — are backsiphonage-only, and no amount of mounting
            height changes that. Where backpressure is possible you need a double check for a low hazard, or a
            reduced pressure principle assembly for a high one.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ==== HAZARD ==== -->
      <section id="hazard" class="bg-slate-50 border-y border-slate-200">
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900">
            Hazard class — pollution or contamination
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
            Once you know the direction, the hazard class narrows it to one device. The IPC's own words are
            <strong> pollution</strong> and <strong>contamination</strong>; the trade says low and high hazard
            and means the same thing.
          </p>

          <div class="mt-8 space-y-5">
            {
              BACKFLOW_HAZARD_CLASSES.map((h) => (
                <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
                  <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
                    <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900">{h.label}</h3>
                    <span
                      class={`rounded-full px-3 py-1 text-xs font-semibold ${
                        h.key === "high"
                          ? "bg-rose-100 text-rose-700"
                          : "bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700"
                      }`}
                    >
                      Code term: {h.codeTerm}
                    </span>
                  </div>
                  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">{h.description}</p>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </div>

          <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
            When the two are combined, only{" "}
            <strong>{highHazardBackpressure.map((a) => a.abbr).join(", ")}</strong> cover a high hazard under
            backpressure — and of those, the air gap is not an in-line device at all. That is why the RPZ ends
            up on so many commercial jobs: for a genuine high-hazard connection under pressure, there is
            nothing else.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ==== ASSEMBLIES ==== -->
      <section id="assemblies" class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900">
          The assemblies, least to most protective
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
          Read the two middle columns first. "Continuous pressure" means the device may sit under supply
          pressure for more than twelve hours at a time — the column that catches people out, because{" "}
          {notContinuous.map((a) => a.abbr).join(" and ")} may not, and a shutoff valve downstream of one is
          the classic installation fault.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-sm">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-200 text-slate-500 bg-slate-50">
                <th class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold">Assembly</th>
                <th class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold">Standard</th>
                <th class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold">Backsiphonage</th>
                <th class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold">Backpressure</th>
                <th class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold">Continuous pressure</th>
                <th class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold">Max hazard</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-slate-700">
              {
                BACKFLOW_ASSEMBLIES.map((a, index) => (
                  <tr class={index === BACKFLOW_ASSEMBLIES.length - 1 ? "" : "border-b border-slate-100"}>
                    <td class="py-3 px-5">
                      <span class="font-semibold text-slate-900">{a.abbr}</span>
                      <span class="block text-xs text-slate-500">{a.name}</span>
                    </td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-5 text-xs">{a.standard}</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-5">
                      <span class="font-semibold text-emerald-600">Yes</span>
                    </td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-5">
                      <span class={a.backpressure ? "font-semibold text-emerald-600" : "font-semibold text-rose-600"}>
                        {a.backpressure ? "Yes" : "No"}
                      </span>
                    </td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-5">
                      <span class={a.continuousPressure ? "text-slate-700" : "font-semibold text-rose-600"}>
                        {a.continuousPressure ? "Yes" : "No"}
                      </span>
                    </td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-5">
                      <span
                        class={`rounded-full px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-semibold ${
                          a.hazard === "high"
                            ? "bg-rose-100 text-rose-700"
                            : "bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700"
                        }`}
                      >
                        {a.hazard === "high" ? "High" : "Low"}
                      </span>
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-10 grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-5">
          {
            BACKFLOW_ASSEMBLIES.map((a) => (
              <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
                <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-2">
                  <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900">{a.abbr}</h3>
                  <span class="text-sm text-slate-500">{a.name}</span>
                </div>
                <p class="mt-1 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-orange-600">
                  {a.standard}
                </p>
                <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">{a.notes}</p>
                <p class="mt-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-3 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                  <span class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Installation:</span> {a.installation}
                </p>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ==== AIR GAPS ==== -->
      <section id="air-gaps" class="bg-slate-50 border-y border-slate-200">
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Minimum air gaps</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
            The whole table is one rule: <strong>{AIR_GAP_MULTIPLIER}× the effective opening</strong>, or{" "}
            <strong>{AIR_GAP_MULTIPLIER_NEAR_WALL}×</strong> where the outlet sits close to a wall, with a
            floor of {fmt(AIR_GAP_MINIMUM_IN, 0)} inch and {fmt(AIR_GAP_MINIMUM_NEAR_WALL_IN, 1)} inches
            respectively. The values below are <em>computed</em> from that rule rather than transcribed, so
            they cannot disagree with it.
          </p>

          <div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-sm">
            <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
              <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-slate-200 text-slate-500 bg-slate-50">
                  <th class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold">Effective opening</th>
                  <th class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold">Away from a wall</th>
                  <th class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold">Close to a wall</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="text-slate-700">
                {
                  MINIMUM_AIR_GAPS.map((row, index) => (
                    <tr class={index === MINIMUM_AIR_GAPS.length - 1 ? "" : "border-b border-slate-100"}>
                      <td class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold text-slate-900">{row.label} in</td>
                      <td class="py-3 px-5">{fmt(row.awayFromWall, 2)} in</td>
                      <td class="py-3 px-5">{fmt(row.nearWall, 2)} in</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-8 grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5">
            <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Measured from the flood level rim</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                The gap runs from the lowest point of the outlet down to the rim water would spill over — not
                to the normal water line, and not to the bottom of the fixture. A faucet spout that swings
                below the rim has no air gap at all while it is there.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Effective opening, not pipe size</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                For an odd-shaped outlet, the effective opening is the diameter of a circle of the same area.
                That is what gets multiplied — so a wide flat outlet needs a bigger gap than its nominal
                connection size would suggest.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Nothing to test, nothing to fail</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                An air gap has no moving parts, needs no annual test, and cannot be defeated by a stuck check
                or a tired spring. Where a fixture can take one, it is the better answer — which is why the
                code reaches for a device only when an air gap is impractical.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">It costs you the pressure</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                The trade-off is that an air gap breaks the pressurised line completely. Anything downstream
                has to run on gravity or be re-pumped, which is exactly why air gaps live at fixtures and
                tanks rather than mid-run on a distribution main.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ==== APPLICATIONS ==== -->
      <section id="applications" class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900">
          What each common connection takes
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
          These pairings follow directly from the direction-and-hazard matrix above. They are the usual answer,
          not a substitute for the purveyor's own cross-connection control programme — which is frequently
          stricter, and which is the body that actually approves the installation.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-sm">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-200 text-slate-500 bg-slate-50">
                <th class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold">Connection</th>
                <th class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold">Direction</th>
                <th class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold">Hazard</th>
                <th class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold">Usual assembly</th>
                <th class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold">Why</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-slate-700">
              {
                BACKFLOW_APPLICATIONS.map((row, index) => (
                  <tr class={index === BACKFLOW_APPLICATIONS.length - 1 ? "" : "border-b border-slate-100"}>
                    <td class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold text-slate-900">{row.application}</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-5">{row.direction}</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-5">
                      <span
                        class={`rounded-full px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-semibold ${
                          row.hazard === "high"
                            ? "bg-rose-100 text-rose-700"
                            : "bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700"
                        }`}
                      >
                        {row.hazard === "high" ? "High" : "Low"}
                      </span>
                    </td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-5 font-semibold text-slate-900">{row.assembly}</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-5 text-slate-600">{row.why}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-6">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">A backwater valve is not a backflow preventer</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
            The names are close enough that they get swapped constantly, but they solve unrelated problems. A{" "}
            <strong>backflow preventer</strong> protects the potable <em>water supply</em> from contamination
            flowing back into it. A <strong>backwater valve</strong> is a check valve in a <em>drain</em>,
            stopping sewage backing into a basement when the public sewer surcharges. Neither does the other's
            job, and fitting one where the other belongs leaves the actual hazard untouched.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ==== TESTING ==== -->
      <section id="testing" class="bg-slate-50 border-y border-slate-200">
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Testing and approval</h2>

          <div class="mt-8 space-y-5">
            <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Testable assemblies are tested annually</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                Anything with test cocks — the PVB, SVB, DC, DCDA, RPZ and RPDA — is a <em>testable assembly</em>
                and is normally required to be tested when installed, when repaired or relocated, and at least
                once a year after that, by a certified tester. The AVB and hose connection vacuum breaker have
                no test cocks; they are devices, not assemblies, and are replaced rather than tested.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Approval is a listing, not a size</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                An assembly has to be on the approved list your purveyor recognises — commonly the USC
                Foundation for Cross-Connection Control and Hydraulic Research listing, alongside the ASSE
                standard for the type. A device that meets the standard on paper but is not on the list will
                still fail inspection, so confirm the listing before ordering rather than after.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Plan for the relief port</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                An RPZ discharges to atmosphere when it does its job, and a failed first check can dump a
                serious volume. It needs an air-gapped drain of adequate capacity, and it may not be installed
                below grade or anywhere it could be submerged. This is the detail most often left out of a
                quote and discovered on site.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Freezing kills assemblies</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                A PVB or RPZ on an irrigation line sits above grade with water in it. In a freezing climate it
                has to be drained down for winter or housed in a heated enclosure — a cracked bonnet in spring
                is the most common irrigation callback there is.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="mt-8 text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
            <strong>Sources &amp; standards:</strong> <a
              class="underline hover:text-orange-600"
              href="https://codes.iccsafe.org/codes/i-codes"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener">IPC</a
            > 2021 Section 608 (protection of potable water supply), including the air gap provisions and the
            application of backflow preventers, with device designations from the <a
              class="underline hover:text-orange-600"
              href="https://www.asse-plumbing.org/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener">ASSE</a
            > standards named in each entry and ASME A112.1.2 for air gaps. The air gap figures on this page are
            computed from the {AIR_GAP_MULTIPLIER}× and {AIR_GAP_MULTIPLIER_NEAR_WALL}× rule rather than
            transcribed. <strong
              >This is a summary for planning, not a substitute for the code text or for your water
              purveyor's cross-connection control programme</strong
            > — purveyor requirements are frequently stricter than the model code, approved-assembly lists vary,
            and roughly fifteen states use the UPC or a derivative instead of the IPC. Testing and certification
            requirements are set locally. A licensed plumber, a certified tester and the AHJ have final say on
            anything installed.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ==== FOOTER NAV ==== -->
      <section class="py-14">
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 text-center">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-slate-900">Keep going</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600">
            A pressure-reducing valve or backflow preventer closes the system — which is what makes the next
            calculation necessary.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
            <a
              href="/calculators/plumbing/plumbing-expansion-tank-calculator"
              class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-xl bg-orange-600 px-6 py-3 text-sm font-bold text-white hover:bg-orange-700 transition"
            >
              Thermal Expansion Tank Calculator →
            </a>
            <a
